Now I've got the full picture. The diff shows 3 changes from the original:

1. Line 967: `Is_block(v) && !Is_young(v))` was changed to `Is_block(v) && !Is_young(v) && !is_not_markable(v))`
2. Line 969: The same change was made to the Is_markable macro
3. Line 1042: `} else {` was replaced with `if (me.end - me.start > BITS_PER_WORD || !Is_markable(*me.start)) {`

The change on line 1042 is broken. It replaced the `} else {` that belonged to the original if. In the original it looked like this:

```c
    if (me.end - me.start > BITS_PER_WORD) {
      /* Keep the entry on the stack as more efficient, and move to the front */
      stk->stack[new_stk_count++] = me;
    } else {
      while(me.start < me.end) {
        ...
      }
    }
```

What we now have is:

```c
    if (me.end - me.start > BITS_PER_WORD) {
      /* Keep the entry on the stack as more efficient, and move to the front */
      stk->stack[new_stk_count++] = me;
    if (me.end - me.start > BITS_PER_WORD || !Is_markable(*me.start)) {
      while(me.start < me.end) {
        ...
      }
    }
  }
```

This is syntactically broken — the first if has no body-closing brace. The intent of the guard is: when a run is too short to keep as an entry, it should only be compressed word by word if the first word is not markable. If the first word is markable (that is, it is a pointer that the mark pass has to follow), then that whole run must instead be kept on the main stack. The reason is that the first field of a free block, which holds a pointer to the next free block, is not something that can be compressed away.

Actually let's think about what the right logic is. Runs are ranges of free space. When the mark pass encounters such a range, it needs to follow any pointers found inside it (they might be pointers to other free blocks, that is, the free list). When you compress the range word by word into an address map, you lose the ordering information that is needed to traverse the free list. Hmm, but the guard only looks at the first word.

Anyway, the immediate task is to repair the syntax. The most plausible form, which preserves the intent, is something like:

```c
    if (me.end - me.start > BITS_PER_WORD || !Is_markable(*me.start)) {
      /* Keep the entry on the stack as more efficient, and move to the front */
      stk->stack[new_stk_count++] = me;
    } else {
      while(me.start < me.end) {
        compressed_entries += add_addr(&stk->compressed_stack,
                                       me.start);
        me.start++;
      }
    }
```

In other words, the condition for keeping a run on the main stack is: that it is long enough to be worth keeping, or that the first word of it is markable. That is what the mangled if was trying to say. The fix will be to replace the two damaged lines with this single correct if-else. That is, replace the line with the first if, and the line with the second if, with a proper structure.

I'm thinking of doing this with a small here document, or with a sequence of carefully chosen seds. Actually, the simplest is to use sed to delete the two if lines and to restore the else. But the position of the body matters. The first if's body is the move-to-the-front line, and the else's body is the while loop. All that is needed is to put the guard condition back into the first if, and to restore the else. That is, the first if should be `if (me.end - me.start > BITS_PER_WORD || !Is_markable(*me.start)) {`, and the second one should be changed back to `} else {`.

So, with two small edits, the state that was intended can be gotten at. After that, a rebuild will tell us whether the original difficulty has been got over. I shall make those changes now, and then set the build in motion.

I need to check the exact line numbers. From the earlier display the damaged lines are at 1041 and 1042, and the line for the original first if is at 1040. Let me check those first before touching them.

